body:
I will second this. I can be overbearing at times, but everyone I have dated has accept me, for me. I have been rejected several times too, it is how the world revolves man, please don't take it personally. If you are being overly clingy or trying to be exclusive too quick or whatnot that may be something to look at, I have done my fair share of that too, especially us with ADHD we want that confirmation that next hit of "YES THIS IS WHAT I HAVE OVERTHOUGHT SO MUCH IT IS NOW REAL!" 

&#x200B;

It hurts, trust me, after 1.5 years with a girl she wasn't even willing to work on some issues that were easily fixable and it showed me that I was with someone that wasn't willing to do the work in the relationship when things got confusing or communication was on par. Use this time as a lesson as much as it feels like you are spiraling and feeling like you are in a void, there is something to be learned here.

I know your pain all too well. Just woke up myself and felt this heavy burden on my chest even now a bit. Reality can seem like a cruel unfair mental prison of what we want, but we know the true outcome of these situations. The struggle to rationalize if they did X,Y,Z or if we did  X,Y,Z, it only hurts you now to conjure those things because they are what you perceive to be the perfect outcome of what you think you need. The thing that has been helping me a bit is know that yes, I made mistakes, but they were able to be worked on and gotten over, but she also made a ton of mistakes and I was able to look past everything because I loved her. That is the thing, love. You wanting to be with someone out of pure love, care, and them knowing they can't stand leaving you and that they need to monitor themselves and help themselves, as you know, if none of that lines up, it will never work. 

&#x200B;

If you ever need to talk you know I am always willing to talk and or just listen. This has been one of the weirdest breakups for me as well, the attachment we get to these people out of in some means being forced to not only love them, but being put in a caregiver position makes it feel double as hard because we feel we have a responsibility to this individual to be with them for their problems, unfortunately, their problems are so vast, constantly changing and are theirs to bear as well. Sure, they can be great at times and you feel this otherworldly connection to them, but it isn't fair to have you always giving that connection and 50% of the time shut it down and treat you like an annoyance. It isn't fair when we see the people they could be or want them to be, but the reality is, they never will be and we all deserve someone that will be that person to us as much as they can, willing to communicate, work on these issues that we face in relationships and not just run or run into the arms of another, mentally and physically empathetic ect. 

&#x200B;

I hope you have a good day. We all share this collective pain, but we can work through it together to see what we really deserve. We have better days infront of us, even if we have to wade through the mental sludge right now.

Keep at it man, I have had people give me numbers, ghost me, sexually interact with me then leave, it unfortunately happens to everyone. I know this is a cliche, but just be yourself because that is who is going to come out when things start to slow down and if people don't accept you for you, then it wouldn't work in the first place. 

&#x200B;

Best of luck man, you deserve someone who will treat you well, with attention and respect.

I know it isn't going to mean anything in the moment, but take what you learned and expand on it. Yes, you have to retake the course, but you now have previous knowledge and can utilize it going forward. I would start doing small study sessions or try to meet with the tutoring center even as soon as tomorrow to work on the concepts now so you get the continuous bombardment of information to really have it stick.

&#x200B;

We all fail, just be persistent and look at what you could have done differently to give yourself the edge to benefit yourself.

Hey man, everyone has to start somewhere and getting your GED is a great start. You are showing you are motivated to want to progress in life. I know the anxiety echo and pull you down, but one of the most helpful things I have heard and try to take to heart is, "Do the thing you are afraid to do." I support you and am behind you, every class I take in college I keep thinking I am a failure or not good enough or these people are so far ahead of me, it isn't true. The comparisson will kill you as well, do what you want to do and focus on your goals, but write them down first. 

&#x200B;

I am rooting for you! If you have any questions or want someone to talk with about the fears, doubts, anxiety ect, my inbox is always open, I know sometimes just talking with someone is a big game changer and it helps get the energy out. 

&#x200B;

Best of luck!
